    {
      "filename": "Former Post Office in Richmond, Badge over entrance.jpg",
      "title": "File:Former Post Office in Richmond, Badge over entrance.jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f1/Former_Post_Office_in_Richmond%2C_Badge_over_entrance.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Former_Post_Office_in_Richmond,_Badge_over_entrance.jpg",
      "width": 2500,
      "height": 2068,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0",
      "artist": "AndyScott",
      "description": "Former Post Office in Richmond, showing the coat of arms of the Municipal Borough of Richmond (Surrey), granted on 19 June 1891. The arms is per fess gules and azure on a fess ermine between in chief a lion passant guardant between two portcullises or and in base a swan argent upon water proper; a representation of the ancient Palace of Richmond proper between two roses gules barbed and seeded proper.\nThe crest was a stag regardant proper, holding in its mouth two roses on one stem, one argent, the other gules, and supporting with the dexter fore hoof a shield or a wreath vert.\nThe portcullises, roses and lion were all associated with King Henry VII, who brought the rival houses of Lancaster and York together and helped build the palace. The swan represented the River Thames. The stag represented Richmond Park and Old Deer Park, and the wreath the idea of municipality.\n\nToday the arms may still be seen in five places in Richmond: on the sign of the pub called The Richmond Arms in Princes Street; in the façade of the former post office in George Street; in the façade of the Old Town Hall in Whittaker Avenue, next to the clock above the entrance; on the Richmond War Memorial, which is near the Old Town Hall; and above the proscenium arch in the Richmond Theatre. There is also an example on display at the Museum of Richmond. (Source: w:Municipal Borough of Richmond (Surrey)",
      "dateCreated": "2018-09-24 12:45:51",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "richmond-palace-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "gcpu"
    },
